-- Q12270: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 270-10-45-6, when a LIFO liquidation occurs during an interim period but is expected to be replaced by year-end, the entity must not reflect the interim liquidation benefit. COGS should include the expected replacement cost, effectively adding $180,000 back to prevent overstating interim earnings.
Wrong (A): Reporting the $180,000 reduction without adjustment would overstate interim gross profit from a temporary inventory fluctuation that management expects to reverse.
Wrong (B): Disclosure alone without adjusting COGS still overstates interim gross profit; ASC 270 requires the cost adjustment, not merely disclosure of expected replenishment.
Wrong (C): Deferring the amount as a liability has no basis under ASC 270; the standard requires adjusting COGS to replacement cost, not creating a deferred liability.$EXPL$
